referenceSolutions.python:
def islandLedgersTotalReceipt(links):
    a=links
    out=[]
    n=len(a)
    seen=[False]*n
    for start in range(n):
        if seen[start]: continue
        stack=[start]
        seen[start]=True
        count=0
        while stack:
            u=stack.pop()
            count+=1
            for v in range(n):
                if a[u][v] and not seen[v]:
                    seen[v]=True
                    stack.append(v)
        out.append(count)
    return sum(out)

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int islandLedgersTotalReceipt(int[][] links) {
    int[][] a=links; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int n=a.length;boolean[]seen=new boolean[n];for(int start=0;start<n;start++){if(seen[start])continue;Deque<Integer>stack=new ArrayDeque<>();stack.push(start);seen[start]=true;int count=0;while(!stack.isEmpty()){int u=stack.pop();count++;for(int v=0;v<n;v++)if(a[u][v]!=0&&!seen[v]){seen[v]=true;stack.push(v);}}out.add(count);}}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)answer+=x;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build componentSizes

This graph has two-way `links` between points. A group, also called a connected component, contains all points that can reach one another. Start at point 0, then check the other points in number order. Whenever a point has not been visited, explore its whole group depth first: follow a path as far as you can before going back. Record how many points are in that group. `componentSizes` follows the order in which you first find the groups. A graph with no points gives an empty `componentSizes`.

Return the answer

Add up every number in `componentSizes`. Store the sum in `total`. Complete `islandLedgersTotalReceipt` so it returns `total`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `componentSizes` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(V^2); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length; V is the number of graph points. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

